== Product information ==
{{PSNote}}

The '''Process Framework''' in Public 360° is a configurable process platform that standardizes and automates end-to-end case handling for public-sector organizations. It provides a single, productized foundation for processes—with different consumption and delivery models layered on top—so customers get consistent structure, compliance, and flow without bespoke development.

'''In short:''' One platform – multiple ways to consume value.

=== What it is ===
* A '''productized process platform''' embedded in Public 360°
* A standardized yet configurable foundation for end-to-end processes (phases, milestones, rules, automation)
* A platform that includes a curated set of '''process model templates''' and process engine templates to speed up adoption
* A starting point, not a blank canvas: customers get ready-made structures they can configure and extend
* A value multiplier for other Public 360° capabilities (e-services, documents, integrations, AI)

=== What it is not ===
* A one-off consulting framework
* A fully bespoke process development service
* Just a template library (templates are included, but value comes from the platform + execution + rules)
* A single-purpose workflow engine

=== Core idea: Individual Standard ===
''Individual Standard'' is the central promise of the Process Framework. Public-sector organizations need:
* Standard, commercially supported solutions with SaaS-like benefits (predictable upgrades, security, compliance)
* The ability to support their specific processes, integrations, metadata, and regulatory context

The Process Framework combines the benefits of standard software with the flexibility required for individual and segment-specific needs—without reverting to bespoke development or upgrade debt.

== Core components ==
The Process Framework is built around three tightly integrated components that form the execution heart of the platform:

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! Component
! Purpose
! Wiki / documentation
|-
| '''Process Models'''
| Define '''what''' the process looks like: structure, phases (stages), milestones, and transitions. Reusable, versioned templates tied to case types.
| [[Process Framework - Process models|Process Framework – Process models]] (functional description)
|-
| '''Process Engine'''
| Execute '''how''' the process runs: orchestration of tasks, automation, integrations, and user actions across the case lifecycle.
| [[360° Process Engine|360° Process Engine]] (module page)
|-
| '''Business Rules Engine (BRE)'''
| Decide '''when''' actions are allowed or required: no-code/low-code rules, event- and time-based triggers, compliance and constraints.
| Described in [[Flexibility initiatives]]; dedicated BRE documentation to be added when released.
|}

'''Process model templates''' (case/process templates) and '''stage templates''' are part of the adoption accelerators delivered with the platform—reusable building blocks that reduce implementation time and support standardization.

Together, these components enable standardized, automated, and compliant end-to-end processes without custom code that breaks upgrades.

== Value propositions ==
* '''Standardization without rigidity''' – Configure and extend within the platform; no upgrade debt.
* '''Automation without bespoke development''' – Rules, deadlines, and flow are built into the process.
* '''Compliance by design''' – Rules and deadlines embedded in workflows; consistent treatment and traceability.
* '''Faster time-to-market''' – Start from standard process and engine templates; add only what you need.
* '''Lower total cost of ownership''' – Predictable upgrades, shared improvements, no long-term maintenance burden from custom code.
* '''One platform, multiple consumption models''' – From single process delivery to full platform access and hosted processes.

== Consumption and delivery models ==
The Process Framework can be consumed in different ways (details depend on commercial offering and market):
* '''Single process delivery''' – One end-to-end process delivered and operated on the platform; low barrier to entry.
* '''Process model framework (configurable)''' – Access to the framework and standard process templates; self-service configuration and extension within the platform.
* '''Hosted processes (standardized)''' – Predefined processes delivered as a managed service with limited configuration; fast onboarding and predictable operation.
* '''Full platform access''' – Full access to Process Framework capabilities including self-service process modeling and governance.

One platform; multiple pricing and consumption models; clear upgrade paths as organizations grow or mature.

== Who this is for ==
* Public-sector organizations with regulated, repeatable case handling
* Organizations that want standardization and flexibility without dependency on custom development
* Large agencies, directorates, and municipalities (full platform or hosted); smaller organizations via single-process or hosted offerings

